  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law caps residential rent increases at 5 percent per 12-month period, exempting units built in the last 15 years. This measure repeals that limit, allowing landlords to raise rent by any amount given 60 days' written notice.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Landlords benefit by gaining the ability to set market-rate prices without caps. Existing tenants bear the cost by facing potentially unlimited rent increases, which may result in higher housing expenses or the financial and personal costs of relocating.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Landlords will likely increase rents more frequently or by larger amounts in high-demand markets. This pricing freedom could create incentives to invest in rental property maintenance and new housing construction. Tenants facing large increases may have to absorb higher living costs or relocate, potentially increasing housing turnover.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses the problem of price controls, which can distort market incentives, discourage housing supply, and reduce property maintenance. The mechanism reaches this by fully deregulating rent prices. However, removing the cap also removes protections against rapid price shocks and unequal bargaining power, exposing tenants to sudden, unaffordable rent hikes and potential displacement.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "Landlords gain significant pricing power and discretion over rent levels and tenant retention.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q7",
      "answer": "The effects of repealing rent control are empirically disputed in terms of overall welfare. Economic findings often show that removing rent limits encourages the long-term supply and maintenance of rental housing by restoring market incentives. Conversely, other researchers find that repealing rent stabilization leads to the immediate displacement of lower-income renters and increased housing instability, without ensuring that new supply lowers rents for vulnerable populations.",
      "disputed": true
    }
  ],
